add('The integral Mordell equation y²=x³−2 has a complete elementary proof','globally complete source','2026-10-09','A','Integral points on a Mordell curve',
'Can the retrieved integral-point list be proved globally instead of justified only by a finite search?',
'The source proposes y²=x³−2 with integral points (3,±5). This particular completeness statement can be proved independently using a Euclidean quadratic order. The argument is special to this equation and does not certify the larger historical frontier of Mordell curves.',
['Work in R=Z[√−2] with positive norm N(a+b√−2)=a²+2b². Units are ±1.','A norm-Euclidean ring admits unique factorization: Euclidean division gives a gcd algorithm, irreducibles are prime, and factorization follows by decreasing norm.'],
'Complete integral solution set',
'The integer solutions of y²=x³−2 are exactly (x,y)=(3,5),(3,−5). Under x=5n−7 these become n=2 and y=±5.',
'First x must be odd: if x were even, y²+2≡0 mod4 would require a square congruent to2, impossible. Then y is odd. The ring R is norm-Euclidean: for any element of its fraction field, round the real coefficients in a+b√−2 to nearest integers; the remainder has norm at most1/4+2/4=3/4<1. Thus R is a UFD. Factor x³=(y+√−2)(y−√−2). A common divisor has norm dividing both the odd number x³ and N(2√−2)=8, so it is a unit. Unique factorization forces each coprime factor to be a cube, with a possible unit absorbed because both units are cubes. Write y+√−2=(a+b√−2)³. Comparing the √−2 coefficient gives1=b(3a²−2b²). Hence b=1 or−1. For b=1, a²=1; for b=−1,3a²=1 has no integer solution. The real coefficient is y=a³−6ab²=−5a, so y=±5. Norms give x=N(a+b√−2)=3. Direct substitution proves both points occur.',
'Transport the complete solution list through the affine restriction.',
'For x=5n−7, the unique possible x=3 gives n=(3+7)/5=2. Conversely n=2 gives x=3 and both signs of y solve the equation. No residue filter or bounded search is needed to exclude other n because the source curve has already been classified globally.',
'This historical two-point list now has a self-contained global proof and an exact restriction transport.',
'The argument depends on unique factorization and the coprimality established for this norm equation. It does not prove that all Mordell curves have similarly small solution sets, nor verify claimed rank witnesses or ClassList modules elsewhere.',
'For y²=x³−D, determine the correct quadratic order, analyze common ideal factors and its class group, and replace element cubing by ideal-theoretic descent when unique factorization fails.',
[])
add('Elliptic multiplication fibres are kernel cosets, with halving checked by a quartic','multiplication fibres as kernel','2026-10-06','A','Elliptic group law',
'Which part of rational preimage closure is automatic from the group law, and where does arithmetic divisibility remain?',
'The recovered halving and tripling discussion correctly points toward kernel cosets and possible missing rational preimages. A general group argument proves the fibre structure. The explicit doubling formula supplies an exact rational witness while showing why a quartic root alone is not a complete lift.',
['Let E:y²=x³+ax+b be nonsingular over a field K of characteristic different from2 and3. Write O for its identity.','For n≥1, [n] denotes multiplication in E(K), and E(K)[n] its rational n-torsion subgroup.'],
'Fibre-coset theorem and halving candidate equation',
'If [n]P₀=Q, then [n]⁻¹(Q)∩E(K)=P₀+E(K)[n]; otherwise the fibre is empty. For a finite P=(u,v) with v≠0 and x(2P)=X, u satisfies u⁴−4Xu³−2au²−(8b+4aX)u+a²−4bX=0.',
'For any other rational preimage P, the homomorphism law gives [n](P−P₀)=O, so P−P₀ is rational n-torsion. Conversely adding such a torsion point preserves the image; translation is a bijection, proving the coset theorem. For doubling, the tangent slope is m=(3u²+a)/(2v), obtained by differentiating the curve equation. Intersecting the tangent line with the cubic and reflecting the third point gives X=m²−2u. Substituting v²=u³+au+b and clearing its nonzero denominator yields X=(u⁴−2au²−8bu+a²)/[4(u³+au+b)], which rearranges to the quartic. A root u must still admit v∈K, have a valid denominator and yield the prescribed y-coordinate of Q; the x-coordinate equation also accepts −Q.',
'Use E:y²=x³−2 and P=(3,5).',
'The slope is27/10, giving 2P=(129/100,−383/1000). Both coordinates satisfy the curve exactly, and the original P supplies a rational half of this Q. The rational2-torsion is trivial because y=0 would require a rational cube root of2; the rational-root theorem excludes one. Therefore this Q has exactly one rational half.',
'The fibre is characterized exactly, and a nontrivial rational doubling example is independently replayed.',
'Having rational Q does not imply it has a rational n-preimage. A bounded saturation computation proves only its stated bound unless linked to a global divisibility theorem; generic tripling claims require their actual rational maps and exceptional cases.',
'For a supplied finitely generated subgroup, compute rational multiplication fibres with exact factorization and lifting checks, then state which primes and index bounds are sufficient for a complete saturation theorem.',
[('Bernstein and Lange: explicit Weierstrass formulas','https://www.hyperelliptic.org/EFD/g1p/auto-shortw.html')])
add('The elliptic logarithmic constant log4 follows from an exact integral split','K(1-z)','2026-10-06','A','Elliptic integral asymptotics',
'Which convention yields K(1−z)+½log z→log4, and can its constant be derived without fitting?',
'The recovered limit is correct for the parameter convention of the complete elliptic integral. A symmetric change of variables reduces its asymptotic to an elementary inverse hyperbolic sine, yielding both the logarithmic divergence and its finite constant.',
['Use the parameter convention K(m)=∫₀^(π/2)(1−m sin²θ)^(−1/2)dθ for0<m<1. In the DLMF modulus convention this is K_DLMF(√m).','Let z∈(0,1), s=√z and L=s^(−1/2).'],
'Parameter-normalized logarithmic asymptotic',
'K(1−z)+½log z→log4 as z↓0. More explicitly, |K(1−z)−2asinh(z^(−1/4))|≤√z/2, which already determines the limit.',
'The substitution t=tanθ gives K(1−z)=∫₀^∞dt/[√(1+t²)√(1+s²t²)]. The inversion t=1/(su) interchanges the intervals (0,L) and (L,∞) and preserves the integrand times its differential. Hence the integral is twice its restriction to(0,L). Its difference from2∫₀^Ldt/√(1+t²)=2asinh L is nonpositive. Using0≤1−(1+u)^(−1/2)≤u/2 gives absolute difference at most s²∫₀^L t²/√(1+t²)dt. Since t²/√(1+t²)≤t, this is at most s²L²/2=s/2. Finally2asinh L=2log(L+√(1+L²))=2log(2L)+o(1)=log4−log s+o(1). Because log s=½log z, the limit follows with its exact constant.',
'Take z=10⁻⁸ and keep a certified remainder.',
'Then L=100 and the difference between K(1−10⁻⁸) and2asinh100 has magnitude at most5·10⁻⁵. No numerical integration is required for this enclosure. The elementary expression also gives the leading approximation log4−½log(10⁻⁸). Indeed0≤2asinh100−2log200≤1/20000, using √(1+u)≤1+u/2 and log(1+u)≤u. Combining this nonnegative correction with the nonpositive split error puts K minus its leading logarithm in[−1/20000,1/20000]. The constant is therefore fixed together with an explicit numerical enclosure. The inequality is also nonasymptotic: at z=1/16 it gives2asinh2−1/8≤K(15/16)≤2asinh2. Thus the same proof yields usable enclosures away from the limiting endpoint, without assuming z is infinitesimal.',
'The convention, divergence and finite constant are all fixed by a direct proof.',
'Writing K for a modulus instead of a parameter changes the argument and the apparent logarithmic constant unless translated correctly. This local asymptotic is not a global period-comparison or arithmetic-motive identification.',
'Expand the symmetric finite integral to further orders with an explicit remainder, then compare its constant term against a separately normalized Picard–Fuchs basis and a fixed transport path.',
[('NIST DLMF: elliptic integral asymptotics','https://dlmf.nist.gov/19.12'),('NIST DLMF: elliptic integral conventions','https://dlmf.nist.gov/19.2')])
add('A sufficiently divisible small integer determinant must vanish','modular divisibility forcing','2026-10-07','A','Exact determinant methods',
'What quantitative inequality turns congruence information about a determinant into an exact algebraic relation?',
'The source discusses integral kernels, divisibility and determinant-zero arguments. The essential step is a strict comparison between a proved integer divisibility modulus and a proved archimedean bound. Without both, a determinant method supplies a candidate relation rather than a certificate.',
['Let A be an r×r integer matrix. Let M≥1 be an integer with M dividing det A.','For its row vectors v_i, Hadamard’s bound is |det A|≤∏_i||v_i||₂. A simpler entry bound |A_ij|≤B gives |det A|≤r^(r/2)Bʳ.'],
'Divisibility–size vanishing certificate',
'If M>|det A| as bounded independently, then det A=0. In particular M>∏||v_i||₂ suffices. Equality of the bound with M is inconclusive, since det A may be ±M.',
'Write det A=Mk with k∈Z. If |det A|<M then |k|<1, forcing k=0. For completeness, Hadamard’s bound follows by Gram–Schmidt on the real row vectors: elementary orthogonalization preserves determinant magnitude, the resulting orthogonal row lengths do not exceed the original lengths, and the determinant magnitude of the orthogonal matrix is their product. Thus an exact upper bound can be derived from rational squared row norms without approximating the determinant. Congruence information must establish divisibility of the determinant itself; entrywise patterns often do this by subtracting rows or extracting column factors, but their powers must be justified. Once a nonzero integer relation among evaluation columns is found, it must be checked on the intended points and its global scope proved separately.',
'Construct an exact relation on a parabola.',
'Evaluate the functions1,x,y,x² at the four integer points (0,0),(1,1),(2,4),(3,9). The last two columns of the resulting matrix agree, so its determinant vanishes and the exact kernel vector (0,0,1,−1) records y−x²=0. A separate sharpness example A=[[M,0],[0,1]] has determinant M: M-divisibility and an upper bound of M do not force zero.',
'The criterion states the exact strict inequality needed for determinant vanishing and checks a recovered kernel relation.',
'A relation found on finitely many points need not hold on an entire curve without a degree/intersection or structural argument. Historical claims such as8² dividing a particular determinant require the actual matrix for replay.',
'For a given bounded integer-point set, build a rational evaluation matrix, prove its p-adic determinant valuation and archimedean bound independently, and use a degree argument to turn the certified kernel into a complete auxiliary-curve covering.',
[])
add('Expected surplus and probability of profit require different dependence assumptions','Money down','2026-10-16','A','Elementary probability models',
'Which parts of the recovered three-item example are exact arithmetic, and what can marginal sale probabilities actually determine?',
'The dated example gives three costs7.25, a shared cost2 and revenue10 per sale. Treating these as a hypothetical finite probability model yields an exact expectation threshold. It does not determine the probability of a positive outcome without information about dependence.',
['Let X₁,X₂,X₃∈{0,1} indicate three sales, S=X₁+X₂+X₃ and Π=10S−95/4.','Assume only equal marginal probabilities P(X_i=1)=p. Independence, when invoked, is an additional explicit hypothesis.'],
'Expectation threshold and sharp dependence bounds',
'EΠ=30p−95/4, so positive expected surplus is equivalent to p>19/24. Profit occurs exactly when all three items sell. Its probability satisfies max(0,3p−2)≤P(Π>0)≤p; under independence it is p³.',
'The total fixed cost is3·29/4+2=95/4. Linearity of expectation gives E S=3p without any independence assumption, proving the expected-value formula and threshold. Since two sales return20<95/4 and three return30>95/4, the positive event is the intersection of the three sale events. An intersection cannot exceed any individual event, proving the upper bound. The union bound on the three failures gives P(all sales)=1−P(at least one failure)≥1−3(1−p)=3p−2, together with nonnegativity. These bounds are sharp: perfect positive dependence gives the upper bound, while suitably disjoint failure events give the lower bound when p≥2/3; analogous constructions give zero for smaller p. Independence instead multiplies the marginal probabilities.',
'Use p=4/5.',
'The expected surplus is24−95/4=1/4. The probability of profit can nevertheless range from2/5 to4/5 under different dependence structures; independence gives64/125. The four realized surpluses for S=0,1,2,3 are−95/4,−55/4,−15/4,25/4, matching the recovered arithmetic.',
'The expectation calculation is exact, while the uncertainty not specified by marginal probabilities is quantified.',
'This is a hypothetical arithmetic model, not a market forecast or recommendation. It omits changing costs, recoverable unsold inventory and alternative objectives; no empirical sale probability is inferred from the historical example.',
'Given an explicit joint uncertainty model, optimize a stated objective such as worst-case loss or probability of positive surplus; compare it with expected-value optimization and certify which dependence assumptions drive any changed decision.',
[])
